<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · War and march · c. 500 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Ballad of Mulan</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="lzh">木蘭詩</bdi> / <bdi lang="lzh-Latn">Mùlán shī</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old Chinese song about Mulan, who dressed as a man to go to war in her father's place and came home a hero.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>northern China under the Northern Wei; the Xianbei-ruled north</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classical Chinese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>northern China (not known exactly)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>believed composed under the Northern Wei (386–535 CE); first written down in the 6th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous folk song, believed to have been composed under the Northern Wei (386–535 CE))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballad-of-mulan">The Ballad of Mulan</a> is the first known written record of Mulan, the legendary heroine who took her aged father's place in the army by disguising herself as a man. It is a folk song believed to have been composed during the Northern Wei regime (386–535 CE). It was first transcribed in the Musical Records of Old and New, a compilation of books and songs made by the monk Zhijiang under the Southern Chen dynasty (557–589) in the 6th century. That anthology is lost; the earliest surviving text of the poem is in the Music Bureau Collection (Yuefu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>), an anthology of the 11th or 12th century whose compiler, Guo Maoqian, names the Musical Records of Old and New as his source.</p><p>The ballad does not state its historical setting, but its geographic and cultural references point to the Northern Wei, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba clan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, a proto-Mongolic people from the northern steppes who united northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 4th century and moved their capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> (modern Datong) to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. Its emperors bore both the Chinese title &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a>&quot; and the nomadic title &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a>&quot;, and the ballad calls the sovereign by both. The office it says the Khagan offered Mulan, shangshulang, was a ministerial post in the highest executive organ of the state. The Black Mountain and the Yanran Mountain that the ballad names are both cited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Book of Wei</a>, the dynasty's official history, for Emperor Taiwu's expedition of 429 against the Rouran, nomads who raided the northern frontier and whom the Northern Wei called &quot;wriggling worms&quot;. Xianbei women were typically skilled riders, and another Northern Wei folk poem, &quot;Li Bo's Younger Sister&quot;, praises a woman's riding and archery, so the ballad may reflect the gender roles of nomadic societies. Mulan's name may be a sinified form of the Xianbei word &quot;umran&quot;, &quot;prosperous&quot;. Whether she ever lived is unclear: her name is not in Exemplary Women, the collection of biographies of women of the Northern Wei.</p><p>The story was retold for centuries. The playwright Xu Wei (d. 1593) dramatised it in two acts as &quot;The Heroine Mulan Goes to War in Her Father's Place&quot; and gave the heroine the family name Hua (&quot;flower&quot;), the one most used today; other versions call her Zhu or Wei. Chu Renhuo's novel Romance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang (c. 1675) moved her to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> and gave her a tragic end, found in no earlier version, after the khan summons her to become his concubine; some commentators have read it as an anti-Qing message. Over time the story grew into a popular folk tale.</p><p>In the 20th century Mulan went onto the stage and the screen: Mei Lanfang starred in the play Mulan Joins the Army in 1917, and silent films followed in 1927 and 1928. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> she became a symbol of unity and of resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s aggression, notably in the 1939 film Mulan Joins the Army, directed by Bu Wancang and written by Ouyang Yuqian. Maxine Hong Kingston retold the tale in The Woman Warrior (1975), which popularised it in the West and may have led to Disney's animated film Mulan of 1998. A crater on Venus is named Hua Mulan after her.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A narrative poem in the plain style of the folk song: a daughter at her loom learns that her father has been called up, takes his place, rides to the northern frontier and serves through long years of war, then refuses high office and asks only to go home, where she puts on her own clothes again and astonishes her comrades. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> are duty to one's parents, courage and the gap between how a person looks and what she can do; the mood moves from quiet worry to the speed of the campaign and the joy of homecoming.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Classical Chinese</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">唧唧復唧唧，木蘭當戶織。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">不聞機杼聲，惟聞女叹息。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">問女何所思？問女何所憶？</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">女亦無所思，女亦無所憶。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">昨夜見軍帖，可汗大點兵。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">軍書十二卷，卷卷有爺名。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">阿爺無大兒，木蘭無長兄。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">願為市鞍馬，從此替爺征。</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">東市買駿馬，西市買鞍韉。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">南市買轡頭，北市買長鞭。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">朝一作「旦」辭爺娘去，暮宿黃河邊。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">不聞爺娘喚女聲，但聞黃河流水鳴濺濺。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">旦辭黃河去，暮至黑山一作「水」頭。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">不聞爺娘喚女聲，但聞燕山胡騎聲一作「</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">」啾啾。</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">萬里赴戎機，關山度若飛。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">朔氣傳金柝，寒光照鐵衣。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">將軍百戰死，壯士十年歸。</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">歸來見天子，天子坐明堂。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">策勳十二轉，賞賜一作「賜物」百千强。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">可汗問所欲，「木蘭不用一作「願」尚書郎。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">願借明駝千里足，送兒還故鄉。」</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">爺娘聞女來，出郭相扶將。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">阿姊聞妹來，當戶理紅妝。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">小弟聞姊來，磨刀霍霍向豬羊。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">開我東閣門，坐我西閣床。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">脫我戰時袍，著我舊時裳。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">當窗理雲鬢，對一作「掛」鏡貼花黃。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">出門看伙一作「火」伴，伙一作「火」伴皆一作「始」驚惶一作「忙」：</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">「同行十二年，不知木蘭是女郎」。</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">雄兔腳撲朔，雌兔眼迷離。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">兩一作「雙」兔傍地走，安能辨我是雄雌？</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sound of one sigh after another,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>As Mulan weaves at the doorway.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>No sound of the loom and shuttle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Only that of the girl lamenting.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ask her of whom she thinks,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ask her for whom she longs.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;There is no one I think of,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>There is no one I long for.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Last night I saw the army notice,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Khan is calling a great draft –</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>A dozen volumes of battle rolls,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Each one with my father's name.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>My father has no grown-up son,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And I have no elder brother.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I'm willing to buy a horse and saddle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To go to battle in my father's place.&quot;</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 62 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>She buys a fine steed at the east market;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>A saddle and blanket at the west market;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>A bridle at the south market;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And a long whip at the north market.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>She takes leave of her parents at dawn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To camp beside the Yellow River at dusk.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>No sound of her parents hailing their girl,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Just the rumbling waters of the Yellow River.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>She leaves the Yellow River at dawn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To reach the Black Mountains by dusk.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>No sound of her parents hailing their girl,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Just the cries of barbarian cavalry in the Yan hills.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ten thousand miles she rode in war,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Crossing passes and mountains as if on a wing.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>On the northern air comes the sentry's gong,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cold light shines on her coat of steel.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The general dead after a hundred battles,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The warriors return after ten years.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>They return to see the Son of Heaven,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who sits in the Hall of Brilliance.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The rolls of merit spin a dozen times,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rewards in the hundreds and thousands.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Khan asks her what she desires,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;I've no need for the post of a gentleman official,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I ask for the swiftest horse,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To carry me back to my hometown.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Her parents hearing their girl returns,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Out to the suburbs to welcome her back.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Elder sister hearing her sister returns,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Adjusts her rouge by the doorway.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Little brother hearing his sister returns,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sharpens his knife for pigs and lamb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;I open my east chamber door,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And sit on my west chamber bed.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I take off my battle cloak,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And put on my old-time clothes.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I adjust my wispy hair at the window sill,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And apply my bisque makeup by the mirror.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I step out to see my comrades-in-arms,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>They are all surprised and astounded:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>'We travelled twelve years together,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yet didn't realise Mulan was a lady!'&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The buck bounds here and there,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whilst the doe has narrow eyes.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>But when the two hares run side by side,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>How can you tell the female from the male?</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous Northern Wei folk song, as in the Yuefu Shiji (Music Bureau Collection), juan 25. Text from <a href="https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E6%9C%A8%E8%98%AD%E8%A9%A9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: Wikisource translation (CC BY-SA 3.0). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Translation%3ABallad_of_Mulan" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
